WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.530
This beautiful tuck lace stitch pattern
is a very simple example

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.500
of what we can knit by combining
tuck and needles out of work.

3
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:16.860
It's created by putting needles out of
work on each side of the tuck stitches.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.060
The Stitch World books and some vintage

5
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.260
publications may show the stitch pattern
in a diagram that looks like this.

6
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:29.020
But on the needle bed, how do you
know what needles to put out of work?

7
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.700
The easiest thing is to do a test run.

8
00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.100
Use some scrap yarn and cast on enough

9
00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.540
needles for a few repeats
of the stitch pattern.

10
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:43.740
Cast on over every needle
and knit a couple of rows.

11
00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:49.140
Then engage your punch card or electronics
and set your machine to tuck.

12
00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:54.060
Knit across all the needles
in tuck and observe what happens.

13
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.900
You can see which stitches
are being tucked.

14
00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.120
With

15
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:08.200
this stitch pattern, needles are out of
work on each side of the tucked stitches.

16
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.930
You could transfer or empty these needles
and continue to test your stitch pattern

17
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.480
in your scrap yarn, or
just note which needles need to be out

18
00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.210
of work when you cast
on with your garment yarn.

19
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.780
Using a test run ensures

20
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.920
that you put the correct needles
out of work according to your pattern.

